{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 812 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8022100b257e8f818647a3f3ebe278a085e466448f2b523c2807b528074644fb" :edb/db-before-t 811 :edb/db-after-t 812 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534124 50 #inst "2026-10-11T16:54:04.256Z" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186151954 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $36u + 19v = \\text{gcd}(36,19)$. What is the value of $u+v$?" 13194139534124 17592186045417 false] [17592186151954 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $36u + 19v = \\text{gcd}(36,19).$ What is the value of $u+v?$" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186151954 1074 "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n36 &= \\begin{vmatrix}19 \\cdot 1 & +\\end{vmatrix}17 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 19 &= \\end{vmatrix}17 \\cdot 1\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 2 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 17|= & 2 \\cdot 8\\end{vmatrix}+ \\mid 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(19,36) =1\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, up to (and including) the equation where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n17 &= 36 - 19 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n2 &= 19 - 17 \\\\\n1 &= 17 - 2 \\cdot 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 17 - 2 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 17 - (19 - 17) \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 17 \\cdot 9 - 19 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= [36 - 19] \\cdot 9 - 19 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 9 - 19 \\cdot 17 \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 9 + 19 \\cdot (-17)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=9,v=-17$, and we can write\n\n$$\n1 = 36\\cdot \\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{9} + 19\\cdot (\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{-17})\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nu+v=9+(-17)=-8\n$$" 13194139534124 17592186045417 false] [17592186151954 1074 "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}36 & = & 19\\cdot 1 & + & 17 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 19 & = & 17\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 17 & = & 2\\cdot 8 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(19,36) =1.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, up to (and including) the equation where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17 & =36-19\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2 & =19-17 \\\\ 1 & =17-2\\cdot 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =17-2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =17-(19-17)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =17\\cdot 9-19\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =[36-19]\\cdot 9-19\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =36\\cdot 9-19\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =36\\cdot 9+19\\cdot (-17)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=9,v=-17,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n1 = 36\\cdot \\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{9}  + 19\\cdot (\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{-17}) .\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=9+(-17)=-8.\n$$" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186151981 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $30u + 8v = \\text{gcd}(30,8)$. What is the value of $u+v$?" 13194139534124 17592186045417 false] [17592186151981 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $30u + 8v = \\text{gcd}(30,8).$ What is the value of $u+v?$" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186151981 1074 "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 &= \\begin{vmatrix}8 \\cdot 3 & +\\end{vmatrix}6 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 8 &= \\end{vmatrix}6 \\cdot 1\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 2 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 6|= & 2 \\cdot 3\\end{vmatrix}+ \\mid 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(30,8) =2\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, up to (and including) the equation where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 &= 30 - 8 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n2 &= 8 - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= 8 - 6 \\\\\n&= 8 - (30 - 8 \\cdot 3) \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot 4 - 30 \\\\\n&= 30 \\cdot (-1) + 8 \\cdot 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=-1$ and $v=4$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nu+v=(-1)+4=3\n$$" 13194139534124 17592186045417 false] [17592186151981 1074 "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}30 & = & 8\\cdot 3 & + & 6 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 8 & = & 6\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 6 & = & 2\\cdot 3 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(30,8) =2.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, up to (and including) the equation where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =30-8\\cdot 3 \\\\ 2 & =8-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =8-6 \\\\  & =8-(30-8\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 4-30 \\\\  & =30\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=-1$ and $v=4.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=(-1)+4=3.\n$$" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152163 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410992 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152163 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410994 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152163 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410995 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152163 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410997 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410993 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410996 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152344 1050 720.0 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151019 13194139534124 17592186045417 false] [17592186152752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410985 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152752 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152752 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $43u + 17v = gcd(43, 17)$. What is the value of $u + v$?" 13194139534124 17592186045417 false] [17592186152752 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $43u+17v=\\text{gcd}(43,17).$ What is the value of $u+v?$" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152752 1074 "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}43 & = & 17\\cdot 2 & + & 9 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 17 & = & 9\\cdot 1 & + & 8 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 9 & = & 8\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(17,43)=1.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =43-17\\cdot 2 \\\\ 8 & =17-9 \\\\ 1 & =9-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =9-8 \\\\  & =9-(17-9) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2-17 \\\\  & =[43-17\\cdot 2]\\cdot 2-17 \\\\  & =43\\cdot 2-17\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =43\\cdot 2+17\\cdot (-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=2$ and $v=-5.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=2+(-5)=-3.\n$$" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151253 13194139534124 17592186045417 false] [17592186152791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410991 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152791 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $28u + 16v = gcd(28, 16)$. What is the value of $u + v$?" 13194139534124 17592186045417 false] [17592186152791 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $28u+16v=\\text{gcd}(28,16).$ What is the value of $u+v?$" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186152791 1074 "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}28 & = & 16\\cdot 1 & + & 12 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 16 & = & 12\\cdot 1 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 12 & = & 4\\cdot 3 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(16,28)=4.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =28-16 \\\\ 4 & =16-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =16-12 \\\\  & =16-(28-16) \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2-28 \\\\  & =28\\cdot (-1)+16\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=-1$ and $v=2.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=(-1)+2=1.\n$$" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186153360 1064 "For any two integers $a$ and $b$, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nThis is known as **Bézout's identity**.\n\nTo find the integers $u$ and $v$, we can use the **extended Euclidean algorithm**, which involves the following three steps:\n\n1. Carry out the Euclidean algorithm to find $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$.\n2. Solve for the remainder at each step, up to (and including) the step where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder.\n3. Back-substitute the remainders to reach the equation $au+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$.\n\nFor example, let's use the extended Euclidean algorithm to find integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\n48u+18v= \\textrm{gcd}(48,18)\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** First, we carry out the Euclidean algorithm to find $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18)$.\n$48|=\\begin{vmatrix}18 \\cdot 2 & +\\end{vmatrix}12; ↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 18|=\\end{vmatrix}12 \\cdot 1\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 6 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 12|= & 6 \\cdot 2\\end{vmatrix}+ \\mid 0$\nSo, we find that $\\text{gcd}(48,18) =6$.\n- **Step 2:** We solve for the remainder (the rightmost term) at each step, up to (and including) the step where the greatest common divisor appeared as an integer.\n$12|= 48 - 18 \\cdot 2; 6|= 18 - 12$\n- **Step 3:** Finally, we back-substitute the remainders to reach the equation $48u+18v= 6$.\n$6|= 18 - 12; = 18 - (48 - 18 \\cdot 2); = 18 - 48 + 18 \\cdot 2; = (18 + 18 \\cdot 2) - 48; = 18 \\cdot 3 - 48; = 48 \\cdot (-1) + 18 \\cdot (3)$\n\nTherefore, $u=-1$ and $v=3$." 13194139534124 17592186045417 false] [17592186153360 1064 "For any two integers $a$ and $b,$ there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that \n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b).\n$$\n\nThis is known as **Bézout's identity**. \n\nTo find the integers $u$ and $v,$ we can use the **extended Euclidean algorithm**, which involves the following three steps:\n\n- Carry out the Euclidean algorithm to find $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b).$\n\n- Solve for the remainder at each step, up to (and including) the step where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder.\n\n- Back-substitute the remainders to reach the equation $au+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b).$\n\nFor example, let's use the extended Euclidean algorithm to find integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\n48u+18v= \\textrm{gcd}(48,18).\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** First, we carry out the Euclidean algorithm to find $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}48 & = & 18\\cdot 2 & + & 12 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 18 & = & 12\\cdot 1 & + & 6 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 12 & = & 6\\cdot 2 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we find that $\\text{gcd}(48,18) =6.$\n\n- **Step 2:** We solve for the remainder (the rightmost term) at each step, up to (and including) the step where the greatest common divisor appeared as an integer.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =48-18\\cdot 2 \\\\ 6 & =18-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:** Finally, we back-substitute the remainders to reach the equation $48u+18v= 6.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =18-12 \\\\  & =18-(48-18\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =18-48+18\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(18+18\\cdot 2)-48 \\\\  & =18\\cdot 3-48 \\\\  & =48\\cdot (-1)+18\\cdot (3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=-1$ and $v=3.$" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186153483 1064 "Throughout this lesson we've made use of **Bézout's identity**, which states that for any two integers $a$ and $b$, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nIt can also be shown that any integer combination of $a$ and $b$ is also a multiple of $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$. In other words, for *any* integers $u'$ and $v'$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(a,b) \\mid (au'+bv')\n$$\n\nMoreover, the set of integers\n$\\big\\{au'+bv' \\,:\\, u', v' \\in\\mathbb Z \\big \\}$\ncontains *all* the multiples of $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$.\n\nWe'll make use of these results in future lessons." 13194139534124 17592186045417 false] [17592186153483 1064 "Throughout this lesson we've made use of **Bézout's identity**, which states that for any two integers $a$ and $b,$ there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that \n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b).\n$$\n\nIt can also be shown that any integer combination of $a$ and $b$ is also a multiple of $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b).$ In other words, for *any* integers $u'$ and $v',$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(a,b) \\mid (au'+bv')\n$$\n\nMoreover, the set of integers \n\n$$\n\\big\\{  au'+bv' \\,:\\, u', v' \\in\\mathbb Z \\big \\}\n$$\n\ncontains *all* the multiples of $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b).$\n\nWe'll make use of these results in future lessons." 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186410944 1080 "3" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186410944 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186410944 1083 #uuid "020f98df-ce27-5f83-a183-d790fdb0efb4" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186410945 1080 "2" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186410945 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186410945 1083 #uuid "025792a9-1418-5ed0-bd75-63fdf48048ad" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186410946 1080 "4" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186410946 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186410946 1083 #uuid "047f5cfa-c319-5769-a5d9-38ab5eac2e3f" 13194139534124 17592186045417 true] [17592186410947 1080 "15" 13194139534124 17592186045417 tr